From: fcp@nuance.com (Craig Presson)
Date: Tue, 04 Oct 1994 22:38:57 -0500
Subject: [#94-10-78] "Proof" in math

At 11:56 PM 10/4/94 -0400, extropians@extropy.org wrote:
>Before mathematics gets roboticized, I would expect that theorem verification
>would be automated.
>
>Are there good, general automated systems for writing down and verifying
>proofs?  I've heard of Z, is it used for anything but program verification?
[...]
Lenat made his name in AI with programs that discover theorems as well
as prove them (actually, what they do is generate proofs and test for
new-and-interesting results with a set of heuristics, hence the
name "Eurisko"). 

>Of course this would be on a lower level than mathematicians are used to,
>but I'll bet it would have the same perverse appeal as programming, and
>create it's own subculture of proof-hackers.

Very much lower level; human mathematicians are total arm-wavers
compared to programs. This is why theorem provers are not in wide
use; for most domains, formalizing the proof enough to get the
program started on it is the same or more work as verifying by hand.

There are so many examples of the computer as labor-creating device,
I begin to think that is its main purpose :-)

