From: DEREK@alcor.org
Date:  Wed, 05 Oct 94 14:45:37 
Subject: [#94-10-82] Recursion, science, and God (fwd)

Craig Presson writes:
>At 04:08 PM 10/1/94, DEREK@alcor.org wrote:
>[...]
>>(Who did once unabashedly proclaim to an atheist friend his firm 
>>belief that he would convert said atheist to Christianity within the 
>>space of not more than two years.  This almost exactly a year 
>>before becoming an atheist himself.)
>
>So, did you still try?

Oh yeah, I tried very hard for about six months.  At the end of that 
time, I began to see that I was getting nowhere with him, and that 
our little debates were becoming increasingly frustrating and 
uncomfortable for me.  I cut him off completely after that, and 
retreated into my own little shell of prayer and study.

There are a few interesting notes about my "conversion" for those 
inclined to dissect the particulars.

1)  Right before (literally *minutes* before) I stopped believing, I 
was at my peak as a believer.  I had never had so much faith, never 
been so active, and never been so able to defend belief on an 
intellectual level.

2)  The change of mind was instantaneous and absolute.  During the 
days and minutes prior to the change, I entertained not a single 
serious, conscious doubt.  The change itself took no more than 30 
seconds.  Since the change, I have not once come close to believing 
again.

3)  I was reading *Stranger in a Strange Land* when the change 
occurred.  (I guess maybe I read the words "Thou art god" too 
many times.  :-)

Has anyone out there ever read *ME* by Thomas T. Thomas?  It's 
about a computer program that gains self-awareness.  Originally 
created as a device for recon and espionage, ME (Multiple Entity) 
specialized in slipping into unsuspecting computers, taking over 
spare computing cycles, writing himself into spare slots on the host 
hard disk, insuring that the host's normal functions continued (so as 
to remain undetected), and eventually taking over all processes of 
the host at once.

This strikes me as parallel to my conversion in many ways.  So long 
as I was adamantly Christian, and desirous of peace of mind, those 
tenacious memes of reason and logic could not survive undetected 
in my conscious mind.  So, they were forced to take a less direct 
route, festering below the surface for many months, making subtle 
alterations here and there, implementing important sub-routines, 
creating the necessary operating platform for the more complex 
memes which would later be implemented.

And then, at a time when I suspected nothing, it happened all at 
once.  One minute I'm a complete christian, the next minute I'm a 
complete atheist.  What an impressive coup!

And what a great study in the complexity of "mind."

Derek Ryan

